.TH XPILOT-INFINITY-TRAY 6 "September 2026" "XPilot Infinity"
.SH NAME
xpilot-infinity-tray \- control the local XPilot Infinity server service
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B xpilot-infinity-tray
.SH DESCRIPTION
Run the tray as a normal desktop user. It connects to the installed
xpilot-infinity-server.service system service without starting or stopping it.
The menu shows service state and provides start, stop, map selection and
configuration editing. Required administrative authorization is requested
when performing an operation.
.PP
Selecting a map saves shared service configuration. If the service is running,
it restarts after a normal stop; connected players are disconnected.
The selected map describes saved configuration, not a verified running game.
.PP
Edit configuration opens a private copy in the default plain-text editor.
Save it as UTF-8 without a byte-order mark, then choose Apply saved changes
in the tray. Saving in the editor alone does not change the service.
Applying while running restarts the server. Conflicts leave the copy intact;
resume editing or discard the copy explicitly before starting again.
.PP
The tray uses StatusNotifierItem on X11 and Wayland, with XEmbed support on
X11. If no usable tray host exists, a control window provides the operations.
Exiting the tray or logging out does not stop the server. Use Stop server
to request a normal service shutdown.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/default/xpilot-infinity-server
Shared server options for the packaged system service.
.TP
.I /usr/share/games/xpilot-infinity/maps
Maps supplied by Debian packages. Other installation prefixes may differ.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
The details action displays configuration and log locations and the most
recent operation result. Read Linux service logs with
.BR "journalctl -u xpilot-infinity-server.service" .
If the service is not installed, install the XPilot Infinity package first.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xpilot-infinity-server (6),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR journalctl (1)
